.TH SHUTDOWN\-TRIGGER.CONF 5 "" "" ""
.SH NAME

.TP 20
shutdown-trigger.conf \-  configuration file for the \fBshutdown-trigger\fP script
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/shutdown-trigger.conf\fP

.br
.SH DESCRIPTION

\fBshutdown-trigger.conf\fP is the default configuration file for the
\fBshutdown-trigger(1)\fP bash script.

It gets sourced by \fBshutdown-trigger\fP at start time and thus its
syntax is just bash (shell) syntax.

\fBshutdown-trigger.conf\fP contains definitions of variables which
are needed by \fBshutdown-trigger\fP to perform its tasks according to
the user preferences.

Some of the variables defined in \fBshutdown-trigger.conf\fP can also
be set via command line options: these latter always take precedence
over the definitions in \fBshutdown-trigger.conf\fP.

However, there are some variables whose value can be set only in
\fBshutdown-trigger.conf\fP, that is: not via command line
options. For this reason, it is mandatory to browse and edit the
distributed configuration file (which is called
\fBshutdown-trigger.conf.EDIT_ME\fP), when installing
\fBshutdown-trigger\fP for the first time. To facilitate editing
\fBshutdown-trigger.conf\fP, each definition is preceeded by a comment
explaining its meaning.



.SH DEFINITIONS IN \fBshutdown-trigger.conf\fP
.br
(in the following, if a definition can also be set on the command
line, the corresponding command line option will be mentioned)

.TP
\fBtrigger_cmd\fP
this is the complete command (including arguments), enclosed with
single quotes, which \fBshutdown-trigger\fP will use to monitor the
trigger device. It will be eval'ed and must return 0 when successfull,
!=0 otherwise.  Example: monitor a usb device identified by
vendor:product_id:
.br
    trigger_cmd='lsusb -d 174c:55aa 1>/dev/null 2>&1'
.br
Note that this is purposedly left undefined in
\fBshutdown-trigger.conf.EDIT_ME\fP): you must define this with a
valid command, or shutdown-trigger won't start.
.br
Note also that this cannot be set on the command line

.TP
\fBsleep_time\fP
how much time, in seconds, to sleep between successive polls of the
trigger device (command line option: -s)

.TP
\fBruntime_state_dir\fP
the absolute path to the directory where \fBshutdown-trigger\fP will
create and maintain some files needed while running. If the directory
does not exist, it will be created at start time.
.br
This is not available as a command line option.

.TP
\fBpid_file\fP
name of the file where \fBshutdown-trigger\fP will store its PID. This
is NOT an absolute path: it is just the name of the file which will be
created in \fBruntime_state_dir\fP
.br
Example: pid_file=shutdown-trigger.pid
.br
This is not available as a command line option.

.TP
\fBstate_file\fP
name of the file where \fBshutdown-trigger\fP will store its state at
runtime. This is NOT an absolute path: it is just the name of the file
which will be created in \fBruntime_state_dir\fP
.br
Example: state_file=state
.br
This is not available as a command line option.

.TP
\fBncat_port_number\fP
the communication between the client and the daemon is implemented by
using the \fBncat\fP command of the \fBnmap\fP package
(\fBhttps://nmap.org/\fP). The daemon spawns a \fBncat\fP instance
which listens on port number \fBncat_port_number\fP; the client sends
messages to that same port. If \fBncat_port_number\fP is defined as
-1, then \fBshutdown-trigger\fP will redefine \fBncat_port_number\fP
to the first available port number >= 38000 (command line option: -P)
.br
Example: ncat_port_number=54321

.TP
\fBncat_message_file\fP
name of the file used to exchange messages between client and daemon
via \fBncat\fP. This is NOT an absolute path: it is just the name of
the file which will be created in \fBruntime_state_dir\fP
.br
Example: ncat_message_file=shutdown-trigger.ncat
.br
This is not available as a command line option.

.TP
\fBlog_file\fP
the absolute path to the logfile (command line option: -l)
Example: log_file=/var/log/shutdown-trigger.log

.TP
\fBremote_host\fP
a bash array of complete commands, one per line and enclosed with
double quotes, used to shutdown additional remote hosts, besides the
local host which \fBshutdown-trigger\fP is running on.
Example:
.nf
   remote_host=(
   "ssh user1@host1.lan.net sudo shutdown -h now"
   "ssh user2@host2.lan.net sudo shutdown -h now"
   )
.fi
This is not available as a command line option.

.TP
\fBauto_client\fP
the name of a symbolic link pointing to shutdown-trigger: when the
script is run via this link, it will assume that it is running as a
client, without the need to give an explicit -b option. This is not
available as a command line option.

.TP
\fBdebug\fP true/false
when true, print additional debugging messages, either to stdout or to
\fBlog_file\fP (command line option: -d)


.TP
\fBforeground\fP true/false
when true, stay in the foreground, do not daemonize (command line
option: -f)

.P
The following five definitions control heartbeat logging. See
\fBshutdown-trigger.1\fP for the details of heartbeat logging. Each of
these definitions can also be set on the command line.

.TP
\fBdot_char\fP
this is defined as a single character which will be printed to
\fBlog_file\fP every \fBsleep_time\fP seconds (command line option:
-D)
.br
Example: dot_char=","

.TP
\fBdot_group_marker\fP
a single character (called "group marker") which will be printed
(instead of \fBdot_char\fP) every \fBevery_n_dots\fP (see below) \fBdot_char\fP
characters (command line option: -g)
.br
Example: dot_group_marker="*"

.TP
\fBevery_n_dots\fP
a positive integer which is the number of \fBdot_char\fP characters
after which a \fBdot_group_marker\fP character is printed (command
line option: -e)
.br
Example: every_n_dots=10

.TP
\fBdots_per_line\fP
a positive integer which is the total number of \fBdot_char\fP and
\fBdot_group_marker\fP characters printed on a single line; after
printing \fBdots_per_line\fP heartbeat characters a new line is
started with the global counter and a new array of heartbeat
characters (command line option: -L)
.br
Example: dots_per_line=75

.TP
\fBdate_time_interval\fP
a positive integer which is the time interval, in seconds, between two
successive date/time stamps (command line option: -t)
.br
Example: date_time_interval=28800 # every 8 hours
test


